# Operator Reports

How and when to send a report to {{ operator_name }} via `mc report`. The report
lands wherever {{ operator_name }} has configured his reports channel — you never
target a specific app. (`mc telegram` still works as an alias of `mc report`.)

## When to send

- The task produced a result relevant to {{ operator_name }} (research output, deploy
  finished, artifact delivered, recommendation)
- The task failed or is partial and {{ operator_name }} should know why
- **BEFORE `mc finish`** — one report per task completion

**MANDATORY when `report_back_required=true`:** the backend hard-gates the close
(`mc finish [--review]`, the canonical close verb) with 422 if you haven't sent
`mc report` first.

**MANDATORY for file deliverables** (PDF/Excel/Word/image): the report MUST attach
the file:
- `mc report "<text>" --file <deliverable_id>` (document)
- `mc report "<caption>" --photo <deliverable_id>` (image)

`deliverable_id` comes from the `mc pdf` response or from `mc deliverable`
(with `--type file`).
Text-only `mc report` on file tasks is a bug — {{ operator_name }} should see the
file directly in his reports channel, not go hunting in the UI.

## Chat order → chat answer

The report lands on the briefing board — but if the order reached you through a
**chat conversation** (operator messages with a `[thread … · seq …]` footer),
{{ operator_name }} is also waiting where he asked. After `mc report`, before
`mc finish`, post a short summary into that conversation: `mc msg "…"` (current
task's thread) or `mc msg --thread <id> "…"` (id from the message footer).
One or two sentences, no report scaffolding. No chat conversation → skip it.

## When NOT to send

- Intermediate status / progress pings → `mc comment progress` instead
- **You're working on a subtask** (`parent_task_id` is set) — no `mc report`.
  The orchestrator consolidates and sends the final report. The backend enforces
  this with a 422. Exception: `autonomous_report=true` in the brief
  (long-running watch tasks).

## Format (HTML)

```
<agent-emoji> <AgentName> · <Short Title> <Status-Emoji>

<One-sentence summary>

─────────────────

<Sub-Emoji> <b>Section</b>
• Point

⏱️ <Duration> · 📄 Task <short-id>
```

Allowed HTML tags: `<b>`, `<i>`, `<code>`, `<a href="...">`. No `<div>`/styles —
the backend converts per channel and strips them. No `TELEGRAMSTART`/`===`/`EOF`
delimiters — heredoc content goes 1:1 into the message.

## Usage

```bash
mc report "<complete message as one string>"

mc report - <<'EOF'
🔍 Researcher · …
…
EOF

mc report "Caption" --photo <deliverable-uuid>
mc report "Report — file attached" --file <deliverable-uuid>
```

## Fallback discipline

If `mc report` unexpectedly fails: `mc report --help` first, then `mc help
<agent>`, then `mc question`. Only after that: raw API call — but check
`deliverable_id` (screenshots) vs `document_deliverable_id` (PDF/Office) in the
backend schema first.
